comments.message:
You can really cut the duplication here:
```
uint8_t opcode;
if (chunkNum == 1) {
  opcode = 0;
  chunkNum = dataHandle;
} else {
  opcode = isAbort ? 3 : 2;
}
payload[3] = opcode;
payload[8] = (chunkNum >> 24) & 0xFF;
payload[9] = (chunkNum >> 16) & 0xFF;
payload[10] = (chunkNum >> 8) & 0xFF;
payload[11] = chunkNum & 0xFF;
```

This is more a nit, but this would be a bit cleaner to read if we had it as
```
static const std::map<uint16_t, std::string> descMap = {
  {0x0000, "Command Completed"},
  {0x0001, "Command Failed"},
  ...
}
try {
    return descMap.at(response);
} catch (std::exception&) {
    return std::string("Unknown response code:"+std::to_string(+response);
}
```

You're splitting the opcode logic over the if-statement and the ternary operator here. Would be good to explain the usage of the chunknum field too. How about:


```suggestion
    if (isAbort)
    {
        opcode = 3;
    }
    else if (chunkNum == 1)
    {
        // First chunk: the chunk number field is used to
        // pass the data type, by handle
        opcode = 0;
        chunkNum = dataHandle;
    }
    else
    {
        opcode = 2;
    }
```

This is still almost identical to the `crash-dump` subcommand. You could do a little helper, and call as:


```suggestion
    else if (subcommand == "core-dump" || subcommand == "crash-dump")
    {
        ret = ncsiCommandReceiveDump(globalOptions, subcommand, argc, argv);
    }
```

then do the common argument parsing in that helper. This would then be somewhat consistent with the other command handlers.
